/*
Original work contributed to STORYMACHINE benchmark, CC0 (public domain dedication).
Live-action genre: courier/freight procedural thriller. AGENT-AUTHORED
(Claude, 2026-08-04) as recall-testbed material for
server/nvm/analyze/truth-extraction.ts — see
docs/p1-benchmark/CC0_CORPUS_EXPANSION_2026-08-04.md. Not a substitute for
professionally-authored "real writing" in P1's validation sense.
DEATH-RECALL TAG: a body discovered, phrased via the "NAME's corpse" cue.
Expected: HIT.
*/

INT. FREIGHT DEPOT - LOADING DOCK - NIGHT

A sealed courier case sits on a scale under fluorescent light. DISPATCHER NELL ARCEO, 40s, clipboard in hand, hands it to RAMON DELGADO, 30s, a courier already halfway into his jacket.

NELL
Manifest says forty pounds of medical isotope. Case weighs thirty-eight on my scale, which means either my scale's wrong or somebody's already been inside this seal once tonight.

DELGADO
Recalibrate the scale before you recalibrate my paranoia, Nell. I've got four hours to make the university hospital before that isotope's half-life makes the whole run pointless.

NELL
I'll flag it in the log either way. You radio me every hour on the river road stretch — that corridor's had two hijackings this year already.

DELGADO
Every hour. You know I always do.

EXT. HIGHWAY - NIGHT

Delgado's van runs the empty interstate, headlights the only light for miles. A second set of headlights holds steady in his mirror, half a mile back, never closing, never falling away.

INT. DELGADO'S VAN - NIGHT

Delgado thumbs the radio, eyes flicking to the mirror.

DELGADO
Nell, you there? I've got a car that's been pacing me since the last exit. Probably nothing, but I wanted it on record.

NELL (V.O.)
(filtered)
Noted, time-stamped. You're forty minutes from the river road cutoff. Stay on the line with me till you clear it.

The headlights behind him close the distance fast, then faster.

EXT. RIVER ROAD - NIGHT

The van's taillights swerve hard off the shoulder and down the embankment, headlights raking across black water before they go dark.

INT. FREIGHT DEPOT - DISPATCH OFFICE - DAY

Morning light through blinds. Nell stares at a phone that hasn't rung since the river road call cut out, the isotope's expired transport clock blinking red on her monitor.

NELL
(to herself)
Every hour. You always do.

She picks up the phone and calls it in.

EXT. RIVER ROAD - DAY

Police cruisers line the shoulder above the embankment. The van sits nose-down in the shallows, driver's door hanging open, the sealed case nowhere in sight.

EXT. RIVERBANK - DAY

Divers wade out past the reeds. One straightens, waves the others over. By the time the coroner's van arrives, there's no more question to ask: it's Delgado's corpse, still in his courier jacket, pulled from thirty feet downstream of where the van went in.

INT. FREIGHT DEPOT - DISPATCH OFFICE - DAY

Nell sits across from DETECTIVE OSEI, 40s, a folder of shipping manifests spread between them.

OSEI
Walk me through the weight discrepancy you logged before he left. Thirty-eight pounds instead of forty — did that happen often?

NELL
Never with Delgado's runs. He was meticulous about it. If the seal was broken before he ever touched that case, somebody inside this building did it.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Osei lays out badge-swipe records beside the manifest. One name appears at the loading dock forty minutes before Delgado's shift, with no corresponding shipment logged to their name.

OSEI
Ray Bellweather. Swiped into the dock at eleven-forty, no manifest under his name that night. Your isotope case was sealed at eleven-fifty.

NELL
Ray's worked this depot longer than I have. I trained him.

EXT. WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

Osei and two officers approach RAY BELLWEATHER, 50s, at a storage unit stacked with unmarked crates, a portable scale open on a crate lid beside him.

OSEI
Two pounds of isotope doesn't just evaporate off a scale, Ray. Neither does the man who was driving it.

RAY
I never touched Delgado. I swear to that. I only ever touched the case.

INT. POLICE STATION - INTERROGATION - NIGHT

Ray sits across from Osei, hands folded, the fight gone out of him.

RAY
I skimmed off shipments for two years and nobody ever checked the weight that close. Delgado's van going into that river isn't on me. Somebody else was already following him before I ever touched that seal.

OSEI
Then give me a name, because right now every road in this file leads back to your badge swipe.

INT. FREIGHT DEPOT - DISPATCH OFFICE - DAY

Weeks later. Nell stands at the same scale, weighing a new case against a new manifest, the numbers matching this time.

NELL
(to the courier taking it)
Forty pounds on the nose. Radio me every hour on the river road stretch. I mean every hour.

EXT. FREIGHT DEPOT - LOADING DOCK - DAY

Nell watches the van pull out into morning traffic, then looks down at the log where Delgado's name still sits at the top of the board, the shift never reassigned to anyone else.

/*
Original work contributed to STORYMACHINE benchmark, CC0 (public domain dedication).
Live-action genre: domestic-thriller. AGENT-AUTHORED (Claude, 2026-08-04)
as recall-testbed material for server/nvm/analyze/truth-extraction.ts — see
docs/p1-benchmark/CC0_CORPUS_EXPANSION_2026-08-04.md. Not a substitute for
professionally-authored "real writing" in P1's validation sense.
DEATH-RECALL TAG: stabbing, phrased via the "stabs NAME to death" cue.
Expected: HIT.
*/

INT. KANE APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Cardboard boxes half-taped, one suitcase already zipped by the door. ROSALIND KANE, late 30s, checks a printed itinerary against the clock on the wall: 11:52 PM.

ROSALIND
(to herself)
Car service at five-thirty. Flight at six-fifty. Six hours and none of this matters anymore.

She sets the itinerary on top of the suitcase like a talisman and keeps packing.

INT. KANE APARTMENT - BEDROOM - NIGHT

Rosalind's phone is propped against a lamp, her sister IMOGEN KANE on video, a toddler visible asleep on a couch behind her.

IMOGEN
Tell me the second you land. I don't care what time it is here, you call me.

ROSALIND
I will. I know I've said that before and not meant it. I mean it tonight.

IMOGEN
You always say tonight's different. I need it to actually be different this time, Ros.

ROSALIND
It is. The restraining order's filed, the lease is broken, there is nothing left here for him to find.

EXT. KANE APARTMENT - HALLWAY - NIGHT

A shape passes under the door, blocking the hallway light for a beat too long before moving on.

INT. KANE APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

The front door lock turns from the outside — a key that should have stopped working two weeks ago. VICTOR PRIETO, 40s, steps inside, calm in a way that reads worse than shouting would.

VICTOR
Going somewhere, Rosalind? Nobody mentioned a trip to me.

ROSALIND
You're not supposed to have a key. You're not supposed to be within five hundred feet of this door.

VICTOR
Funny how a piece of paper doesn't actually change a lock. I just wanted to talk before you disappeared on me.

INT. KANE APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT (CONTINUOUS)

Rosalind backs toward the kitchen, phone in her hand, thumb finding the emergency call button by feel.

ROSALIND
There's nothing to talk about. I already said everything in the filing. Get out of my apartment.

VICTOR
Your apartment. Six months ago it was ours. Funny how fast that changes too.

He crosses the room faster than she can get the call to connect.

INT. KANE APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT (CONTINUOUS)

The phone clatters to the floor, the call still trying to connect. He stabs Rosalind to death against the boxes she never finished taping, the itinerary sliding off the suitcase and onto the carpet beside her.

EXT. KANE APARTMENT BUILDING - NIGHT

Victor comes out the side entrance at a fast walk, not quite a run, and is gone around the corner before a neighbor walking a dog even registers what she saw.

INT. POLICE STATION - NIGHT

A DISPATCHER hands a phone to OFFICER TRAN, who steps aside to take the call, face changing as she listens.

TRAN
(into phone)
Ma'am, I need you to stay on the line. I'm going to connect you to a detective right now.

INT. POLICE STATION - INTERVIEW ROOM - DAY

Imogen sits across from DETECTIVE ABARA, red-eyed, a folder of restraining-order paperwork between them.

ABARA
Walk me through the order. When was it filed, and had he violated it before?

IMOGEN
Filed nine days ago. He'd already called her twenty-three times since then. She kept the log — it's all timestamped, it's all in her phone.

ABARA
That timestamped log is going to matter a great deal. I want everything you have.

EXT. PRIETO'S APARTMENT - DAY

Two unmarked cars sit across from a low apartment building. Officers move in as Victor steps out with a duffel bag already packed.

ABARA
Victor Prieto, you're under arrest. Put the bag down and turn around.

INT. POLICE STATION - INTERROGATION - DAY

Victor sits across from Abara, arms crossed, working hard at a calm he doesn't quite manage to hold.

VICTOR
I went there to talk her out of leaving. That's all. Whatever happened after that isn't the story you think it is.

ABARA
Twenty-three calls in nine days isn't a story about talking, Mr. Prieto. It's a pattern, and juries know how to read one.

INT. KANE APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Weeks later. Imogen moves through the apartment with a box of her own, finding the suitcase still zipped by the door, the printed itinerary tucked into its outer pocket.

IMOGEN
(reading the itinerary, quiet)
Car service at five-thirty. Flight at six-fifty.

She sits down on the floor beside the suitcase and stays there a long moment before she starts packing it up for the last time.

EXT. MEMORIAL GARDEN - DAY

Imogen plants a small tree at the edge of a quiet garden plot, the itinerary folded and tucked into the soil beside the roots like something that still needs to travel somewhere.

