INT. BRASSAIA - KITCHEN - LATE AFTERNOON

Prep in full swing. Chef LUCIA REYES, 30s, works a line of tickets clipped above the pass when her estranged father WALT REYES, 60s, ties on an apron at the far station like he's never left.

LUCIA
Desmond hired you without telling me. I want that on record before you touch a single pan in my kitchen.

WALT
He needed a line cook for opening week and I needed work. I didn't know it was your kitchen until I walked in the door, Lucia.

LUCIA
It's my kitchen because you left the old one to rot for twelve years. Don't act surprised it's mine now.

INT. BRASSAIA - WALK-IN COOLER - LATE AFTERNOON

Cold air and hanging cases of produce. Lucia follows Walt in, voice low so it won't carry to the line.

LUCIA
You don't get to show up the week we open and pretend the last twelve years didn't happen.

WALT
I'm not pretending anything. I'm asking for a shift, not a conversation. I'll take whichever one nobody wants.

LUCIA
Fine. Nobody wants the walk-in inventory shift. Enjoy it.

INT. BRASSAIA - KITCHEN - EARLY EVENING

Sous chef DESMOND ABARA, 40s, catches Lucia by the tasting spoons, voice careful.

DESMOND
I should have asked you first. I panicked when Reyes Senior called about a line cook slot and I didn't put together who he was fast enough.

LUCIA
You knew exactly who he was, Desmond. You've heard me talk about him for three years.

DESMOND
I did. And I also know we're two cooks short for opening week and he's the best knife hand I've worked beside in a decade. Both things are true.

INT. BRASSAIA - DINING ROOM - EARLY EVENING

Front-of-house manager RENEE OKAFOR checks a printed reservation sheet against a seating chart.

RENEE
Eight o'clock, table twelve, under a name that isn't real but everyone in this business recognizes anyway. The Sentinel's critic is coming tonight.

DESMOND
On our second night open. Wonderful. No pressure at all.

RENEE
None whatsoever. I just thought the kitchen should know before eight o'clock finds out for you.

INT. BRASSAIA - KITCHEN - EVENING

Tickets fire in fast. Lucia calls orders over the noise while Walt works the far station with an economy of motion that draws a few glances from the younger cooks.

LUCIA
Fire two duck, one risotto, all day!

WALT
(quiet, to his own station)
All day.

INT. BRASSAIA - KITCHEN - EVENING (CONTINUOUS)

A sauce breaks on the line, thin and split under the heat. The cook on that station freezes. Walt steps in without being asked, whisking in cold butter a spoon at a time until it comes back together.

WALT
Off the heat first, always. You can save almost anything off the heat that you'd lose staying on it.

LINE COOK
Thank you, Chef. I didn't know that trick.

Lucia watches from across the pass, saying nothing, filing it away anyway.

INT. BRASSAIA - PASS - EVENING

Lucia and Walt work the same six feet of counter for the first time all night, plating side by side without speaking.

LUCIA
That sauce trick. Grandma taught you that, or you taught her?

WALT
She taught me. Same as she'd have taught you, if I hadn't taken us both away from her kitchen for twelve years.

INT. BRASSAIA - KITCHEN - EVENING

Renee slides a ticket onto the rail, table twelve, three courses ordered in careful sequence.

RENEE
Table twelve just ordered. It's happening. Try not to let anyone see you sweat.

LUCIA
Nobody sweats on my line. We just breathe very deliberately.

INT. BRASSAIA - KITCHEN - EVENING (CONTINUOUS)

Lucia plates the entrée her own way, precise but flat. Walt leans in, unasked, and nudges the sauce line half an inch with the back of a spoon.

WALT
Your grandmother always finished it off-center, like this. Left more room for the eye to land on the protein first.

LUCIA
(a beat, then doing it)
Off-center. Fine. If it doesn't work I'm blaming your twelve-year absence for the bad review.

INT. BRASSAIA - DINING ROOM - NIGHT

Renee delivers the plate to table twelve. The critic studies it a long moment before finally lifting a fork, expression giving away nothing at all.

INT. BRASSAIA - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Service has wound down. Lucia and Walt share the last clean towel between them, wiping down the same station from opposite sides.

LUCIA
The sauce trick worked. So did the plating. I'm not saying that means anything.

WALT
It doesn't have to mean anything tonight. I'll take a good ticket and a shared towel as a start.

EXT. BRASSAIA - ALLEY - NIGHT

Lucia and Walt sit on overturned milk crates by the back door, the kitchen noise finally quiet behind them.

WALT
I know I don't get twelve years back by fixing one sauce. I just wanted you to know I remember all of it, even the parts I wasn't there for.

LUCIA
Same shift tomorrow. We'll see how the sauce holds up on a Friday.

She doesn't smile, but she doesn't get up and leave either, and for tonight that's enough for both of them.

