INT. QUIET PAGE BOOKSTORE - MORNING

MARJORIE ANSON, 50s, straightens a shelf of paperbacks. Her employee TEDDY LOWRY, 20s, sweeps near the register.

MARJORIE
A developer called yesterday. Wants to buy the building, maybe put in a café or something. I'm thinking about it.

TEDDY
Are you going to sell it?

MARJORIE
I don't know yet. I haven't decided.

INT. QUIET PAGE BOOKSTORE - AFTERNOON

Teddy restocks a cart of returns. Marjorie sits behind the register with a mug of tea.

TEDDY
So what happens if you do sell it? Where would you go?

MARJORIE
I'm not sure. I really haven't thought that far ahead. It's a big decision.

TEDDY
Yeah. I guess it would be.

INT. QUIET PAGE BOOKSTORE - MORNING

A REGULAR CUSTOMER browses the poetry section while Marjorie tidies a nearby display.

REGULAR CUSTOMER
I love this place. I hope it's here for a long time.

MARJORIE
Me too. We'll see what happens.

REGULAR CUSTOMER
Well, good luck with whatever you decide.

INT. QUIET PAGE BOOKSTORE - AFTERNOON

Marjorie's phone rings. She steps toward the window to answer it, Teddy half-listening from the counter.

MARJORIE
(into phone)
Hi, yes, I'm still thinking about the offer. No firm answer yet. I'll call you when I've decided.

She hangs up and goes back to straightening the same shelf as before.

INT. QUIET PAGE BOOKSTORE - MORNING

Teddy waters a small plant by the window. Marjorie counts the register drawer.

TEDDY
Any news on the sale thing?

MARJORIE
Not really. Still thinking about it.

TEDDY
Okay. Let me know if you need anything.

INT. QUIET PAGE BOOKSTORE - AFTERNOON

Marjorie's sister CAROL ANSON stops by, browsing a shelf without much urgency.

CAROL
Teddy mentioned the developer. Are you leaning one way or the other?

MARJORIE
Not really. It's a big decision. I want to be careful about it.

CAROL
That makes sense. No rush, I guess.

MARJORIE
No rush.

INT. QUIET PAGE BOOKSTORE - MORNING

Marjorie takes another call, Teddy dusting a shelf nearby.

MARJORIE
(into phone)
Whenever I'm ready is fine? Okay. I appreciate you being patient. I'll be in touch.

She sets the phone down and goes back to what she was doing before it rang.

INT. QUIET PAGE BOOKSTORE - AFTERNOON

Teddy leans on the counter while Marjorie flips through a catalog of new releases.

TEDDY
Have you thought more about the sale?

MARJORIE
A little. It's still on my mind. I go back and forth.

TEDDY
That's understandable. It's a big decision.

INT. QUIET PAGE BOOKSTORE - MORNING

Marjorie stands alone in the empty store before opening, looking around at the shelves.

MARJORIE
(to herself)
A café would probably do fine here. So would a bookstore, I suppose. Either one.

She unlocks the front door for the day and starts the coffee machine behind the counter.

INT. QUIET PAGE BOOKSTORE - AFTERNOON

Teddy is at the register. Marjorie comes down from the small office upstairs.

MARJORIE
I called the developer back. I told them I'm not selling. I think I'd just rather keep things the way they are.

TEDDY
That's great, Marjorie. I'm glad.

MARJORIE
Me too, I think. Anyway. Back to work.

She picks up the same stack of paperbacks from the first scene and starts shelving them again.

/*
Original work contributed to STORYMACHINE benchmark, CC0 (public domain dedication).
Live-action genre: home-invasion thriller. AGENT-AUTHORED (Claude, 2026-08-04)
as recall-testbed material for server/nvm/analyze/truth-extraction.ts — see
docs/p1-benchmark/CC0_CORPUS_EXPANSION_2026-08-04.md. Not a substitute for
professionally-authored "real writing" in P1's validation sense.
DEATH-RECALL TAG: gunshot aftermath, phrased via the "kills NAME" cue.
Expected: HIT. Also contains a post-death FLASHBACK scene (Marcus alive)
to test the extractor's non-literal-scene exclusion guard.
*/

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

A lamp knocked sideways throws long shadows across a room mid-argument. PRIYA OYELARAN, 20s, stands with her phone already dialing. Her older brother MARCUS OYELARAN, 30s, takes it out of her hand and ends the call.

MARCUS
Not yet. If it's nothing, a squad car in the driveway makes us the story on every porch camera on the block by morning.

PRIYA
That thing downstairs is not a raccoon, Marcus. You always do this. You decide first and ask me after.

MARCUS
I decide first because I'm the one who has to explain it to Mom if I'm wrong. Get in your room. Lock it.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - HALLWAY - CONTINUOUS

Marcus pulls a bat from behind the hall closet door. Priya lingers at her doorway, unconvinced, watching him check the stairwell.

PRIYA
At least take my phone. If something happens I want a way to know.

MARCUS
Nothing is happening. Lock the door, Priya. I mean it this time.

She goes in. The latch clicks. Marcus starts down the stairs one at a time, bat low.

EXT. OYELARAN HOUSE - BACK PORCH - NIGHT

The kitchen door's window is broken from the outside in, glass scattered on the mat. A gloved hand reaches through and turns the deadbolt.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

DEV KASPRZAK, 30s, jacket zipped to the chin, is already inside when Marcus rounds the corner. Neither man expected the other to be this close.

MARCUS
You're in the wrong house. Whatever Ellis told you I owe him, it isn't here.

DEV
Ellis didn't send me for money, Marcus. He sent me for the ledger you've been holding over him since March.

MARCUS
There's no ledger. Get out before I make you leave the way you came in.

Dev's hand comes out of his jacket holding a pistol, low and unsteady.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT (CONTINUOUS)

Marcus raises the bat a half-second too late. The shot catches him in the shoulder and he staggers into the counter, refusing to go down, reaching for the drawer behind him.

DEV
Don't. Don't make me do this again.

MARCUS
Priya, stay upstairs—

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT (SECONDS LATER)

The second shot kills Marcus before he can move another step. Dev stands over him a moment, then bolts through the broken door into the dark.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - BEDROOM - NIGHT

Priya, phone pressed to her ear, hears the second shot through the floor and goes still.

PRIYA
(to dispatcher)
Two shots. My brother's downstairs. Please, someone needs to come now.

She's already moving for the door despite everything he told her.

EXT. OYELARAN HOUSE - NIGHT

Red and blue light washes the front lawn. Priya sits on the porch step wrapped in a paramedic's blanket, staring at nothing while officers move in and out of the house behind her.

INT. POLICE STATION - INTERVIEW ROOM - NIGHT

Fluorescent light, a scratched table. DETECTIVE OKAFOR, 50s, slides a legal pad toward Priya, who hasn't touched the coffee in front of her.

OKAFOR
Take your time. I know how tonight went. I just need what you remember, in order, as close to exact as you can get it.

PRIYA
He said "not yet" and "get in your room" and then he went down the stairs by himself, because that's what he always does. That's the order.

OKAFOR
Did your brother know somebody named Ellis? Or a Kasprzak?

PRIYA
Ellis owed him money for a job neither of them will admit was under the table. I don't know a Kasprzak.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY (FLASHBACK)

Sunlight instead of shadow. A YOUNGER PRIYA, twelve, stands on a step stool at the counter while MARCUS, twenties, guides her hands over a mixing bowl.

MARCUS
Fold it, don't stir it. Stirring knocks the air back out — you'll spend an hour getting it back in.

YOUNGER PRIYA
You're just making that up so I do it your way.

MARCUS
Maybe. Try it my way once and tell me I'm wrong.

He steals a fingerful of batter off the spoon before she can stop him. She swats his arm, laughing.

INT. POLICE STATION - HALLWAY - DAY

Okafor meets Priya outside the interview room, folder under his arm.

OKAFOR
Ellis rolled on him inside an hour. Kasprzak did three debt collections for Ellis this year alone, all off the books. This wasn't random.

PRIYA
So the ledger was real. He never once mentioned it to me.

OKAFOR
People protect the ones they love from the parts that could hurt them. Looks like that's what got him killed.

EXT. KASPRZAK'S APARTMENT COMPLEX - DAY

Two unmarked cars flank the entrance. Priya watches from across the street as officers walk Dev out in cuffs, head down, not looking at anyone.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY

Weeks later. Priya stands at the same counter, the same mixing bowl in front of her, folding batter the way she was taught instead of stirring it.

PRIYA
(to herself)
Fold it, don't stir it.

She almost smiles. It's the first time she has, in this room, since the night the window broke.

EXT. OYELARAN HOUSE - DAY

Priya steps out onto the porch where she once sat wrapped in a blanket, coffee in hand this time, and looks at the street like she's deciding whether it's still hers. She decides it is, and goes back inside.

/*
Original work contributed to STORYMACHINE benchmark, CC0 (public domain dedication).
Live-action genre: hotel mystery. Troubled-band craft calibration.
Per band design, richness is held constant (clock, clue, relationship beat all
present); craft fails pervasively: the clock is stated then flatly contradicted
when the deadline passes with no consequence, the clue is seeded and genuinely
orphaned, four consecutive scenes recycle the same beat, there is no revelation,
the two voices stay interchangeable, and the dialogue leans on the on-the-nose
cliche lexicon the engine flags.
Author: STORYMACHINE benchmark contributor, 2026.
*/

INT. HOTEL CORRIDOR - NIGHT

A long, dim stretch of patterned carpet. CLERK NORA, 30s, pushes a housekeeping cart past numbered doors. A brass KEY sits in the cart's tray with a leather tag reading ROOM 12.

NORA
Checkout is at eleven. That's the rule around here.

INT. FRONT DESK - NIGHT

Nora checks the leather-bound guest book under a gooseneck lamp.

NORA
Checkout is at eleven. I feel so sure about that, honestly.

INT. ROOM 12 - NIGHT

Nora lets herself in with the tagged key. On the nightstand, a folded NOTE in unfamiliar handwriting: "Do not open the window."

NORA
"Do not open the window." I'll leave it for the manager. Trust me, it's fine.

INT. BACK OFFICE - NIGHT

Nora and Manager DEL, 50s, speak over a desk piled with invoices. An old grievance surfaces but goes nowhere.

NORA
You reassigned me to nights last summer and never explained why.

DEL
Honestly, I feel so overwhelmed, I just didn't get to it. Everything's fine.

INT. ROOM 12 - NIGHT

Nora tidies the already-tidy room. Nothing changes.

NORA
Same as always in here.

DEL
Same as always.

INT. ROOM 14 - NIGHT

Nora tidies the next room in the same way.

NORA
Same as always.

DEL
Same as always.

INT. ROOM 9 - NIGHT

Nora tidies another identical room.

NORA
Honestly, same as always.

DEL
Same as always, I swear.

INT. LAUNDRY - NIGHT

Nora folds sheets at a long folding table.

NORA
Everything's fine.

DEL
Everything's fine, trust me on this.

INT. FRONT DESK - DAY

Eleven o'clock passes. Nobody checks out. The window of Room 12 is open, curtains moving in a draft. Nothing happens and nobody acts on it.

NORA
The window's open. I guess checkout doesn't matter, honestly.

DEL
It doesn't matter. Everything's fine.

INT. LOBBY - DAY

Nora and Del part at the revolving door. The note in Room 12 was never mentioned again.

NORA
I feel so fine about all of this.

DEL
Same as always. Take care.

FADE OUT.

/*
Original work contributed to STORYMACHINE benchmark, CC0 (public domain dedication).
Live-action genre: environmental procedural thriller. Competent-to-strong craft.
Author: STORYMACHINE benchmark contributor, 2026.
*/

INT. COUNTY WATER DISTRICT LAB - NIGHT

FLUORESCENT light over two long rows of sample bottles, each tagged with a creek-mile number. SARA LIN, 30s, safety glasses up on her forehead, pipettes blue reagent into a vial and caps it. The label reads CREEK MILE 14 - TURBIDITY.

Her supervisor, GUS REINHART, 60s, reading glasses on a chain, hangs up a desk phone and turns to her.

GUS
The state inspector lands Thursday morning, two days from now. That's the deadline. Either we close the intake and prove the water's safe, or the district shuts the whole system down and a hundred thousand people drink bottled until spring.

SARA
The creek isn't clean, Gus. The turbidity alone is triple the federal limit, and I haven't even run the metals panel yet.

GUS
Then prove where the silt's coming from before Thursday. Find a source I can write up, or the state writes us up instead.

EXT. CREEK MILE 14 - DAY

Sara wades in hip boots, a sampling pole in one hand. Upstream, past a stand of birch, a CHAIN-LINK FENCE LINE and a contractor's trailer with a logo she doesn't recognize, white text on green: TIDEWALL GROUP. She photographs both with a dated timestamp.

SARA
(into handheld recorder)
Creek Mile 14, Tuesday morning. Turbidity source appears to originate above the new construction pad at the tree line. The upstream contractor is Tidewall Group. I'm going to need their discharge permit.

INT. DISTRICT RECORDS ROOM - DAY

Sara pulls a PERMIT FOLDER from the cabinet. Inside, the Tidewall Group stormwater discharge permit, stamped APPROVED six weeks ago. The signature on the approval line is one she knows.

She carries the folder into Gus's office and lays it open on his desk.

SARA
Gus. You signed the Tidewall permit. Six weeks ago. Your name is on the approval line.

GUS
The application was complete when it came across my desk. The engineering summary looked sound. I had no reason to hold it.

SARA
And the engineering was wrong, and now there's sediment in the intake that feeds half the county. The truth is, I need you to tell me whether you actually reviewed the calculations, or whether you stamped a summary.

GUS
The truth is, Sara, I was three months behind on a backlog of permits and I read the summary, not the calculations. I initialed it the way I initial everything that crosses my desk when I'm behind.

SARA
There was a citizen complaint on Mile 14 two years ago. Cloudy water after every rain. I found it in the records room tonight. Your intake signature, no action taken.

GUS
I remember that complaint. I triaged it as low priority because we were short-handed and the readings you'd taken that season were within tolerance. I was wrong to let it sit.

INT. TIDEWALL CONSTRUCTION TRAILER - DAY

Sara stands across a folding table from the Tidewall site foreman, JORDAN, 40s, hard hat off, blocking the trailer's back door with his body.

JORDAN
We're permitted and we're within spec. Whatever's in your creek came off somebody else's job.

SARA
Your silt fence failed in the last rain event. I have the turbidity readings, dated, upstream and downstream of your pad. If the state inspector walks that creek on Thursday morning, you lose the contract and the bond.

JORDAN
Thursday's two days. We'll mend the fence and reseed the berm.

SARA
The fence isn't the source, Jordan. Your excavation cut through a clay layer that was sealing off the old groundwater. You're discharging groundwater now, not storm runoff. Mending the fence is window dressing on a structural failure.

Jordan's smile flattens. He glances at the back door.

JORDAN
You take this to the state, Ms. Lin, and I'm the one who answers for it. Not the engineer who drew the plans. Me.

SARA
Then tell me who drew the plans. Give me a name and I'll put it in the report where it belongs.

JORDAN
(after a beat)
The engineering came from the district's own approved template. We built exactly what your office stamped.

He opens the back door and lets it swing shut behind him. Sara photographs the clay layer on the excavation face before she leaves.

INT. DISTRICT RECORDS ROOM - NIGHT

Sara pulls the engineering template folder Gus referenced. Inside, beside the stamped template, an older COMPLAINT FORM dated two years back, filed by a citizen upstream, never assigned, never investigated. The intake signature is Gus's.

SARA
(reading)
"Cloudy water at Mile 14 after every rain." Filed two years ago. Intake by G. Reinhart. Status: no action.

She photographs the form and slides it back into the folder.

INT. DISTRICT LAB - NIGHT

Sara and Gus work side by side under the fume hood, running the metals panel on the Mile 14 samples. An old wound opens between titrations.

SARA
You mentored me for nine years, Gus. You taught me to read a chain of custody before you taught me to drive. And then two years ago you stopped trusting my field readings, and I started second-guessing every result I turned in.

GUS
I stopped trusting your readings because you flagged the Whitfield permit before the politics were ready for it, and the board came down on both of us. I was trying to protect your career by keeping you off the controversial samples. I see now that I was protecting my own.

SARA
The truth is, I would rather have been wrong in public than right in private and unheard. I forgive the silence, Gus. What I don't want is to keep working around you.

GUS
Then we work together, the way we used to. Thursday is the deadline, and we meet it with the data, not the politics. I'll stand behind the numbers whatever the board says.

EXT. CREEK MILE 14 - DAY

Sara wades upstream with a fresh SAMPLE KIT, GPS-tagged bottles in a cooler on the bank. At the excavation face where the clay layer was breached, she collects three controlled samples, caps and seals each, and labels the chain-of-custody form in the rain.

SARA
(into recorder)
Source confirmed. Groundwater intrusion at the excavation face, not stormwater. The permit's discharge category is incorrect. This is the finding the inspector needs Thursday.

INT. DISTRICT BOARDROOM - WEDNESDAY NIGHT

The board chair, VANCE, 60s, holds a press release across the table at Gus. Sara stands behind his chair.

VANCE
If you file that addendum before the inspector lands, you're handing the state a confession. The district takes the liability and Tidewall walks.

GUS
The district already carries the liability, Vance. Filing the addendum only makes it honest. I'd rather the inspector find it on the record than in the creek.

VANCE
And if the board doesn't back you?

GUS
Then I'll stand behind the numbers on my own. But the chair should know the citizen complaint from two years ago is in the same folder, and it has my intake signature on it. The board backs the data, or the data comes out anyway.

Vance sets the press release down. He says nothing. Gus picks up the addendum and walks out with Sara behind him.

INT. COUNTY WATER DISTRICT LAB - THURSDAY MORNING

The STATE INSPECTOR, 50s, blue blazer, reviews Sara's data binder on the bench beside the three sealed samples and the chain-of-custody log. Gus stands a step behind Sara.

STATE INSPECTOR
These samples were collected and sealed under proper chain of custody, with GPS timestamps and upstream-downstream controls. The discharge is mischaracterized in the permit. I'm halting the Tidewall excavation pending a full recategorization.

GUS
The district will support the halt without appeal. The original permit was approved on an incomplete review. That's on me, and I'm filing an addendum to that effect in the record today.

The inspector nods, packs the binder, and leaves. Sara and Gus are alone with the hum of the lab equipment and the bank of sample bottles.

SARA
I'm grateful you chose the data in the end. Thursday came and we made it matter instead of letting it pass.

GUS
I'm grateful you wouldn't let me hide behind the politics the way I'd been hiding for two years. We start the next intake review together, properly this time, and I'll read the calculations, not the summary.

Sara caps the last sample bottle and sets it in the rack with the others. Outside the lab window, the creek runs clear past the stalled construction pad, the deadline met, the system still running.

FADE OUT.

