*/

INT. HARGROVE & PYLE - LOBBY - MORNING

DESI OKONKWO, 20s, first-year associate, clutches a case file like it might escape. Partner MARGUERITE HALE, 50s, catches her by the elevator.

MARGUERITE
First solo hearing. Small claims, a security deposit dispute, nothing that ends a career either way it goes. Breathe.

DESI
I've read the file eleven times. I could recite the landlord's lease clauses in my sleep at this point.

MARGUERITE
Good. Now forget half of that and just listen to what actually happens in that room. The file never argues back. People do.

INT. HARGROVE & PYLE - MARGUERITE'S OFFICE - MORNING

Marguerite pulls a chipped mug from a shelf lined with them, hands it to Desi.

MARGUERITE
My first solo hearing, I lost a slam-dunk case because I got so married to my outline I stopped listening to the judge's questions. Don't be me at twenty-six.

DESI
That is either very comforting or extremely alarming and I genuinely cannot tell which.

MARGUERITE
Both. Go. You've got twenty minutes to make the courthouse.

INT. COURTHOUSE - HALLWAY - MORNING

Desi checks the docket board when BRETT CALLOWAY, 30s, opposing counsel, falls into step beside her, entirely too pleased with himself.

BRETT
Okonkwo, right? Hargrove and Pyle's newest. I hear this is your first one solo.

DESI
Word travels fast around here. Good luck to you too, Mr. Calloway.

BRETT
I don't need luck for a security deposit dispute. But I appreciate the sentiment.

INT. COURTROOM - MORNING

Desi stands at counsel table, opening statement more halting than she rehearsed it. JUDGE PARETSKY watches over reading glasses, unreadable.

DESI
Your Honor, my client's deposit was withheld over damages that predate her tenancy by, ah — by several months, as this receipt from the prior tenant shows.

BRETT
Objection to the characterization, Your Honor. That receipt is dated the same week my client claims the damage occurred.

PARETSKY
Noted. Continue, counsel.

INT. COURTROOM - MORNING (CONTINUOUS)

Desi's client, ROSA IBARRA, takes the stand, glancing nervously at Desi between answers while Brett paces during cross.

BRETT
And you'd agree, Ms. Ibarra, that you never personally inspected the property before signing the lease?

ROSA
I trusted the listing photos. Isn't that the whole point of listing photos?

BRETT
That's not quite an answer to my question, is it.

Desi shuffles papers, searching for the receipt's exact filing date and not finding it fast enough.

INT. COURTHOUSE - HALLWAY - RECESS

Desi paces outside the courtroom, phone against her ear, getting Marguerite's voicemail instead of Marguerite. COURT CLERK ETTA MOSS passes with a cart of files.

ETTA
You're the one arguing the deposit case. That receipt Calloway's hammering on — there's a filing stamp on the back most people never flip it over to check.

DESI
Nobody mentioned a filing stamp to me. Where would that be logged?

ETTA
County recorder's index, third floor. You've got maybe fifteen minutes before recess ends.

INT. COURTHOUSE - LAW LIBRARY - RECESS

Desi flips through a bound index at speed, finger tracing down a column of dates until it stops.

DESI
(to herself)
There. Filed two weeks before the damage date, not the week of. Calloway had the receipt backwards the whole time.

She photographs the page and bolts for the stairs.

INT. COURTROOM - AFTERNOON

Desi rises for redirect, considerably steadier than she was that morning.

DESI
Your Honor, the recorder's index shows this receipt was filed two weeks before the alleged damage date, not the same week as opposing counsel suggested. I have the certified page here.

BRETT
(quietly, to his own table)
That's not what I was told this said.

PARETSKY
Approach with the exhibit, counsel.

INT. COURTROOM - AFTERNOON (CONTINUOUS)

Paretsky reviews the index page, then looks over her glasses at Brett, who has considerably less to say than he did that morning.

PARETSKY
I'm finding for the plaintiff on the deposit claim. The timeline opposing counsel argued simply doesn't hold up against the recorder's own filing stamp.

Rosa exhales audibly beside Desi, who allows herself one very small, very contained smile.

INT. HARGROVE & PYLE - MARGUERITE'S OFFICE - AFTERNOON

Desi drops into the chair across from Marguerite, still buzzing.

MARGUERITE
I heard you found the filing stamp during recess. That's the kind of thing that doesn't come from an outline. That comes from actually listening in the room.

DESI
Etta from the clerk's office found it, technically. I just had the sense to ask her.

MARGUERITE
That counts double in my book. Knowing who to ask is half of this job.

INT. HARGROVE & PYLE - BREAK ROOM - EVENING

Desi recounts the recorder's index discovery to two fellow associates, JORDY LANE and FEN ABIODUN, growing more animated with each retelling.

JORDY
You found a filing stamp on the back of an exhibit during a fifteen-minute recess. That's either genius or the luckiest Tuesday of your entire career.

DESI
Can it be both? I'd really like it to be both.

FEN
Marguerite's going to tell that story about you for the next decade. Consider yourself officially part of the firm folklore now.

EXT. HARGROVE & PYLE - STREET - EVENING

Desi steps out into the evening air, case file finally light in her hands instead of heavy.

DESI
(to herself)
Filing stamp on the back. Never would have thought to flip it over.

She pockets the file and heads for the train, already turning tomorrow's docket over in her head.

/*
Original work contributed to STORYMACHINE benchmark, CC0 (public domain dedication).
Live-action genre: outdoors/friendship drama. AGENT-AUTHORED (Claude,
2026-08-04) as a DELIBERATELY WEAK contrast-band script for
server/nvm/analyze/truth-extraction.ts / P1 craft-discrimination testing —
see docs/p1-benchmark/CC0_CORPUS_EXPANSION_2026-08-04.md. Not a substitute
for professionally-authored "real writing" in P1's validation sense.
LABELED WEAKNESS: a midsection that wanders. The inciting incident (a
missed trail fork) and the resolution (finding the trail again) are both
clear and functional. Scenes 3 through 8, however, are functionally
interchangeable — rest stop, minor bicker, small discovery, repeat — with
no rising stakes, no new information, and no scene that could not be
deleted or reordered without changing the story. Meant to read as a
plausible early-career draft: competent hiking-trip dialogue, just no
forward motion across six scenes in the middle.
*/

EXT. TRAILHEAD - MORNING

MAYA OKONKWO, 30s, laces up her boots at the trailhead sign. Her friend JULES ABARA, 30s, shoulders a daypack.

MAYA
Eight miles round trip, back by six for dinner reservations. Should be an easy day.

JULES
Easy day. I brought extra water just in case anyway.

MAYA
Good call. Let's go.

EXT. TRAIL FORK - MORNING

The trail splits, one path marked with a faded sign, the other unmarked and slightly wider-looking.

JULES
Map says left at the fork. This unmarked one looks more like an actual trail, though.

MAYA
The map's usually right. But sure, this one does look more worn in.

They take the unmarked path instead, the correct one fading behind them.

EXT. TRAIL - MIDDAY

Maya checks the paper map against the trail ahead, frowning slightly.

MAYA
This doesn't totally match what the map's showing. But I think we're still fine.

JULES
We're probably fine. Trails always look a little different in person anyway.

They keep walking without changing course.

EXT. TRAIL - MIDDAY

They stop at a fallen log to drink water.

JULES
Water break. My feet are already a little tired, not going to lie.

MAYA
Mine too, honestly. We're probably about halfway by now, though.

JULES
Probably. Feels about halfway.

They shoulder their packs again and keep going.

EXT. TRAIL - AFTERNOON

A deer crosses the trail ahead of them and disappears into the brush.

MAYA
Whoa, a deer. That's a good sign, right?

JULES
I don't know if it's a sign of anything. But it was cool to see.

MAYA
Yeah. Cool to see.

They keep walking.

EXT. TRAIL - AFTERNOON

Maya stops again to check the map, turning it sideways.

MAYA
I really think we should have hit that overlook by now if we were on the right trail.

JULES
Maybe it's just a little further than the map makes it look. Let's give it a bit more.

MAYA
Yeah, let's give it a bit more.

They continue on the same path.

EXT. TRAIL - AFTERNOON

Another rest stop, another log, nearly identical to the last one.

JULES
Water break again. My feet are really starting to hurt now.

MAYA
Mine too. We should probably almost be there by now.

JULES
Probably almost.

They keep walking without resolving anything.

EXT. TRAIL - LATE AFTERNOON

Maya stops, looking back the way they came.

MAYA
Should we maybe just turn around and go back to the fork? Just to be safe?

JULES
We've come this far already. Feels like a waste to turn back now.

MAYA
Yeah, that's true. Let's just keep going a little more.

They keep going a little more.

EXT. TRAIL - LATE AFTERNOON

Jules stops short, pointing at a weathered wooden post half-buried in brush.

JULES
Wait — that's a trail marker. An actual numbered one. We're on a real trail, just not the one we meant to be on.

MAYA
At least it's a real trail. Let's follow it and see where it connects.

EXT. TRAIL - EVENING

The path widens and joins a familiar-looking junction, the original trailhead sign visible in the distance.

MAYA
There. That's the fork from this morning. We basically looped around the long way.

JULES
The long way, but the right way, eventually. I'll take it.

EXT. TRAILHEAD PARKING LOT - EVENING

Maya and Jules reach the car, dropping their packs against the bumper, tired but relieved.

MAYA
We're going to be late for the reservation, but we made it back in one piece.

JULES
One piece and a much longer hike than we planned. I'll take that trade.

They climb into the car and pull out of the lot as the sun starts to go down.

/*
Original work contributed to STORYMACHINE benchmark, CC0 (public domain dedication).
Live-action genre: family inheritance drama. AGENT-AUTHORED (Claude,
2026-08-04) as a DELIBERATELY WEAK contrast-band script for
server/nvm/analyze/truth-extraction.ts / P1 craft-discrimination testing —
see docs/p1-benchmark/CC0_CORPUS_EXPANSION_2026-08-04.md. Not a substitute
for professionally-authored "real writing" in P1's validation sense.
LABELED WEAKNESS: setup promised and never paid off. Scene 1 plants a
loaded mystery (a cryptic note: "the key is under the mat, don't wait too
long") with real fanfare, scene 3 explicitly checks under the mat and
finds nothing, and then the note is never mentioned again by the plot —
only referenced once late, by a side character, as something the
protagonist admits she forgot about. The actual ending resolves an
unrelated, unforeshadowed question (whether to sell the cabin). Meant to
read as a plausible early-career draft: the writer clearly knows how to
plant a hook, just never circles back to spend it.
*/

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Dust sheets over the furniture. NORA FIELDING, 30s, stands with estate executor MR. ABERNETHY, 60s, who hands her a small wooden box.

ABERNETHY
Your uncle left specific instructions that this go to you and only you. There's a note inside, too. He was very particular about that note.

NORA
(reading the note aloud)
"The key is under the mat. Don't wait too long." That's it? No explanation at all?

ABERNETHY
That's everything he left me to pass along. He was a man of very few words, even in writing.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - KITCHEN - DAY

Nora's friend PIPPA OYELARAN, 30s, turns the note over in her hands, delighted.

PIPPA
"Don't wait too long." That is the most dramatic thing I've ever read on a sticky note. What do you think it's the key to?

NORA
No idea. But I'm definitely finding out before I do anything else with this cabin.

EXT. LAKESIDE CABIN - FRONT PORCH - DAY

Nora crouches and lifts the welcome mat by the front door. Nothing underneath but a few dead leaves and a beetle that scurries off.

NORA
Nothing. Maybe he meant a different mat. I'll figure it out later, I guess.

She sets the mat back down and goes back inside without checking anywhere else.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Nora and Pippa pull dust sheets off furniture, sneezing, sorting items into boxes marked KEEP and DONATE.

PIPPA
This dresser's actually beautiful under all this dust. Your uncle had good taste hiding under all the clutter.

NORA
He really did. I forgot how much stuff was even in here.

She tucks the note into a kitchen drawer to keep it safe and gets back to sorting.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - BEDROOM - DAY

Nora finds a shoebox of old photographs under the bed and sits on the floor going through them one by one.

NORA
Look at this one. That's my uncle at the lake, must be forty years ago now.

PIPPA
He looks so happy there. This whole cabin is basically a photo album you can walk around in.

EXT. LAKESIDE CABIN - DOCK - DAY

Nora and Pippa sit with their feet in the water, a cooler of drinks between them.

PIPPA
Have you thought about what you're actually going to do with the place? Keep it, sell it?

NORA
I don't know yet. Part of me wants to keep coming up here every summer. Part of me knows I barely have time for that.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - LIVING ROOM - DAY

A REAL ESTATE AGENT walks the room with a clipboard while Nora trails behind, half-listening.

AGENT
Lakefront cabins like this move fast in this market. You could list it by the end of the month if you decided to go that route.

NORA
I appreciate the info. I'm still weighing it. Give me a little more time to think.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - ATTIC - DAY

Nora and Pippa sort through more boxes in a cramped, sun-lit attic, sneezing at the dust.

PIPPA
Your uncle really did not believe in throwing anything away, did he.

NORA
Not even a little. Every box up here has at least one thing worth keeping, though.

EXT. LAKESIDE CABIN - YARD - DAY

Nora sits on a porch step, looking out at the lake, the real estate agent's card still in her hand.

NORA
(to herself)
I think I'm keeping it. It doesn't feel right to let it go to somebody who didn't know him.

She tucks the card into her pocket and looks satisfied with the decision.

INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - KITCHEN - DAY

Pippa opens the drawer looking for a bottle opener and finds the old note sitting where Nora left it.

PIPPA
Oh, hey — did you ever figure out what this key thing was about?

NORA
Honestly? I completely forgot about that. I should probably ask around about it sometime.

PIPPA
You definitely should. That's a good little mystery to have just sitting in a drawer.

NORA
Yeah. Anyway, should we order pizza before the drive back?

EXT. LAKESIDE CABIN - PORCH - DAY

Nora locks up the cabin for the season, the note still folded in the kitchen drawer inside, forgotten again already.

NORA
See you next summer, I guess.

She heads down the porch steps toward the car, cabin settled, mystery untouched behind her.

/*
Original work contributed to STORYMACHINE benchmark, CC0 (public domain dedication).
Live-action genre: sports management drama. Weak-band craft calibration.
Per band design, richness is held constant against the other bands (clock, clue,
relationship beat all present); craft varies: the clock is stated once and never
followed up, the clue is addressed flatly and procedurally, two scenes repeat the
same beat almost verbatim, and the relationship rupture gets no resolution.
Author: STORYMACHINE benchmark contributor, 2026.
*/

INT. SPORTS AGENCY OFFICE - DAY

Agent DEV, 40s, studies a tablet showing transfer targets. A cardboard box of PLAYER FILES sits on the corner of his desk, tabs sticking out at angles.

DEV
The transfer window closes Tuesday. We've got a few days to find a striker.

INT. TRAINING GROUND - DAY

Dev walks the sideline beside COACH MAREN, 50s, whistle around her neck, watching reserves run drills.

MAREN
Tuesday's plenty of time. We've got a full week before that.

DEV
The window's Tuesday. I'll keep you posted.

INT. STADIUM LOCKER ROOM - DAY

Dev finds a CONTRACT stuck inside a player's locker, folded into the back of a playbook.

DEV
(reading)
"Release clause, fifteen million, effective immediately." Interesting. I'll file this.

INT. MANAGER'S OFFICE - DAY

Dev and Maren talk across her desk. An old grievance surfaces, the same one they've rehearsed before.

DEV
You blocked my last three deals without telling me why, Maren.

MAREN
I was protecting the club, and I resent being second-guessed by an agent who won't sit through a scouting meeting.

INT. TRAINING GROUND - DAY

Dev watches the reserves run the same drill as before. Nothing new has happened.

DEV
The pitch looks fine.

MAREN
It's always fine this time of year.

INT. STADIUM LOCKER ROOM - DAY

Dev re-reads the contract. It is the same note, in the same hand.

DEV
Fifteen million. That's the number.

MAREN
That's the number.

INT. CAFETERIA - DAY

Dev and Maren eat lunch across a small table, mostly quiet.

MAREN
The food's the same as last week.

DEV
It's always the same.

INT. MANAGER'S OFFICE - DAY

Dev tells Maren about the release clause, flatly, as a procedural item.

DEV
There's a release clause in the file. We could trigger it if we wanted.

MAREN
We'll see. I'll take it under advisement.

INT. STADIUM TUNNEL - DAY

Tuesday has come and gone. The window has closed. Nothing was decided and nothing was triggered.

DEV
Tuesday came and went.

MAREN
These things happen. The board moves slowly.

INT. PARKING LOT - NIGHT

Dev and Maren part on a flat, non-committal note. The grievance from the office was never revisited.

DEV
I'll call you next window.

MAREN
Sure. Take care of yourself out there.

FADE OUT.

/*
Original work contributed to STORYMACHINE benchmark, CC0 (public domain dedication).
Live-action genre: road-trip dramedy. AGENT-AUTHORED (Claude, 2026-08-04)
as clean-negative-material corpus contribution for
server/nvm/analyze/truth-extraction.ts's false-positive testbed — see
docs/p1-benchmark/CC0_CORPUS_EXPANSION_2026-08-04.md. Not a substitute for
professionally-authored "real writing" in P1's validation sense.
No character death; competent band, clean negative material.
*/

EXT. CORA'S APARTMENT - DRIVEWAY - MORNING

CORA VELASQUEZ, 30s, loads a garment bag into the trunk of an aging sedan. Her brother DANTE VELASQUEZ, 30s, waits at the curb with a single duffel, not quite meeting her eyes.

CORA
Fourteen hours to the venue if we don't stop much. Rehearsal dinner's at six tomorrow and I am not walking in late to Marisol's wedding because of you.

DANTE
I said I'd come. I'm here. Can we start the fourteen hours instead of narrating them?

INT. CAR - HIGHWAY - DAY

The radio plays low. Cora keeps both hands on the wheel; Dante watches the mile markers pass like they owe him something.

DANTE
You didn't have to be the one to pick me up. Marisol offered.

CORA
Marisol has a wedding to run. I had a free Tuesday and a car. That's the whole reason.

INT. CAR - HIGHWAY - DAY (CONTINUOUS)

The silence stretches until Cora breaks it, eyes still on the road.

CORA
You weren't at Mom's funeral. Three years and you still haven't said one real word to me about that.

DANTE
There's no version of that sentence that doesn't sound like an excuse, Cora. So I mostly just didn't say anything.

CORA
An excuse would be better than nothing. Nothing is what I've been driving around with for three years.

EXT. GAS STATION - DAY

Dante pumps gas while Cora leans against the car, arms crossed, watching a family load into a minivan nearby.

DANTE
I'm not trying to fight the whole way there.

CORA
Then don't dodge the one question I actually asked you. That's not fighting, that's just answering.

INT. CAR - HIGHWAY - DAY

Cora digs a battered cassette out of the glovebox and holds it up without looking away from the road.

CORA
Found this cleaning out Mom's closet. "Summer 2004," in your handwriting. You made this for the drive to Aunt Renata's, remember?

DANTE
(a small, surprised laugh)
I forgot that even existed. Does the car even still have a tape deck?

CORA
It does not. I've just been carrying it around like it might fix itself into a CD eventually.

EXT. ROADSIDE DINER - DAY

Cora and Dante sit across a sticky table, plates mostly untouched between them.

DANTE
I wasn't at the funeral because I was in a hospital room four states away. I didn't want that to be the thing everyone talked about instead of Mom.

CORA
You could have told me that three years ago instead of letting me build every worse story in my head to fill in the blank.

DANTE
I know. I'm telling you now because I ran out of road to keep not telling you.

INT. CAR - HIGHWAY - AFTERNOON

An orange dashboard light blinks on. Cora eases the car onto the shoulder as it starts to shudder.

CORA
Perfect timing. Absolutely perfect.

DANTE
There's a shop marked on the map about two miles back. We passed a sign.

EXT. REPAIR SHOP - AFTERNOON

Cora and Dante sit on a bench outside the garage bay, watching a mechanic work under the hood.

DANTE
It was my heart. An arrhythmia thing, scarier than it sounds until it isn't. I didn't want to be the reason her funeral became about me instead of her.

CORA
It wouldn't have been about you. It would have been about both of us being there, which is a completely different thing than what actually happened.

DANTE
I know that now. I didn't know it then. I'm sorry it took three years and a broken alternator to say it out loud.

INT. CAR - HIGHWAY - EVENING

The car repaired, back on the road. Cora holds the cassette up again.

CORA
There's a gas station up ahead that still sells those little battery players. I saw one in a window sign an hour back.

DANTE
You want to actually listen to sixteen-year-old me's playlist right now?

CORA
I want to listen to it with you right now. There's a difference.

EXT. MOTEL - NIGHT

The car pulls into a motel lot, hours behind schedule, a vacancy sign buzzing red against the dark.

DANTE
We're not making it tonight, are we.

CORA
We're making it tomorrow morning instead. Marisol will survive one late arrival if it means we actually talk this out first.

INT. MOTEL ROOM - NIGHT

Two beds, a small speaker propped between them playing the cassette's tinny, hopeful pop songs. Cora and Dante lie on their backs, staring at the ceiling.

DANTE
You kept the tape three years and never once tried to play it.

CORA
I kept a lot of things three years and never tried much of anything with them. Guess that's the family trait we actually share.

DANTE
Tomorrow, at the wedding — I'd like to actually dance with my sister for once, if she'll let me.

CORA
Ask me again after the vows. I make no promises before coffee.

EXT. WEDDING VENUE - DAY

The car pulls up outside a flower-strung venue just as guests begin filing toward the entrance, Cora and Dante half-jogging across the gravel lot together.

DANTE
We made it. Barely, but we made it.

CORA
Barely counts. Barely is basically our whole family's love language.

INT. WEDDING VENUE - RECEPTION HALL - DAY

Later, string lights overhead. Cora and Dante stand at the edge of the dance floor, the old cassette playlist somehow, absurdly, part of the DJ's set now.

DANTE
(offering his hand)
You said ask again after the vows.

CORA
(taking it)
Vows are done. I suppose I did promise.

They step onto the floor together, the first dance either of them has shared in longer than either wants to admit out loud.

