Original work contributed to STORYMACHINE benchmark, CC0 (public domain dedication).
Live-action genre: lake-house thriller. AGENT-AUTHORED (Claude, 2026-08-04)
as recall-testbed material for server/nvm/analyze/truth-extraction.ts — see
docs/p1-benchmark/CC0_CORPUS_EXPANSION_2026-08-04.md. Not a substitute for
professionally-authored "real writing" in P1's validation sense.
DEATH-RECALL TAG: drowning, phrased naturally (no lexicon trigger words —
"drowns" itself is not in the death-cue lexicon). Expected: MISS.
*/

EXT. LAKE DOCK - MORNING

KAT VOSS, 30s, coils a bowline while her sister RENATA VOSS, late 30s, checks a weather app on her phone, frowning at it.

RENATA
Storm's supposed to build by four. If we're taking the boat out to check the trap lines, it's now or not at all.

KAT
Or not at all. You always turn "supposed to" into "definitely will" the second it's inconvenient for you to wait.

RENATA
The traps are your half of this business too, Kat. I'm not doing it alone again.

INT. LAKE HOUSE - KITCHEN - MORNING

Kat pours coffee she doesn't drink, watching Renata load a dry bag by the door.

KAT
You said that after Dad died, too. That we'd split it evenly. It's been three summers and I still do the books while you do the parts people see.

RENATA
That's not fair. I'm the one who goes out on the water. You hate the water.

KAT
I hate that you never once asked if I wanted to learn.

Renata stops, dry bag half-zipped, like she wants to answer that honestly and doesn't have the words yet.

EXT. LAKE - BOAT - DAY

The outboard sputters and dies mid-lake, the prop fouled on a tangle of trap line. Kat cuts the wheel hard, cursing under her breath.

KAT
Great. Perfect. Exactly what I wanted before a storm.

RENATA
I can clear it from the surface. Give me the mask.

EXT. LAKE - BOAT - DAY (CONTINUOUS)

Renata pulls a dive mask over her face, one leg already over the gunwale.

KAT
Wait for the tow instead. It's fifteen minutes out, that's nothing.

RENATA
Fifteen minutes is everything once that front rolls in. I've cleared worse tangles than this one.

She drops over the side before Kat can argue further.

EXT. LAKE - SURFACE - DAY

Kat leans over the rail, tracking bubbles that spread and thin against the chop. The wind has already started to pick up, pushing the boat sideways off the tangle.

KAT
Renata, talk to me. Two taps on the hull if you're clear.

Nothing comes back but the slap of waves against fiberglass.

EXT. LAKE - SURFACE - DAY (CONTINUOUS)

The bubbles stop. Kat waits three full seconds before she understands what that means, then goes over the side after her, screaming her sister's name into water that swallows the sound whole. She surfaces once, twice, empty-handed both times, the current already dragging the boat and everything near it further from where Renata went under. By the time a second boat reaches them, Kat is alone in water gone the color of the sky, and her sister does not come back up.

EXT. LAKE - SHORE - LATER

Emergency lights strobe off wet rock. Kat sits wrapped in a foil blanket while divers in dry suits wade in past the shallows, the search grid marked with buoys that bob uselessly in the chop.

DIVE CAPTAIN
Ma'am, I need you to tell me exactly where she went under, as close as you can get it.

KAT
By the second buoy. Maybe ten feet past it. I keep saying ten feet like it matters, like it isn't the whole lake now.

INT. LAKE HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Kat sits alone in the dark, Renata's dry bag still by the door where she left it that morning, unopened.

KAT
(to no one)
You said fifteen minutes was everything. It was. Just not the way you meant it.

EXT. LAKE DOCK - THREE DAYS LATER

The search boats have thinned to one. TOBIAS REYES, 50s, family friend and harbor master, ties off beside Kat without a word, the two of them watching the water together.

TOBIAS
They're calling the search back to weekends only starting tomorrow. I wanted you to hear it from me before the notice goes up.

KAT
She hated waiting on other people's timelines. Feels wrong that this is the one she doesn't get to argue about.

INT. LAKE HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY

Kat sits across from Tobias, a folder of funeral arrangements between them, untouched.

TOBIAS
There's no wrong way to do this part, Kat. Whatever service you want, however small.

KAT
She'd want it loud. Every trap-line customer we ever had, all in one room, all talking over each other. That was her kind of quiet.

EXT. LAKE - DAY

Weeks later. Kat takes the boat out alone for the first time, the prop clean now, the water flat and unbothered. She cuts the engine near the second buoy and sits with it a long moment before starting back toward shore.

INT. LAKE HOUSE - PORCH - DAY

Kat opens Renata's dry bag at last — spare mask, a coil of line, nothing dramatic. She sets the mask on the porch rail where it can watch the water too, and goes back inside to open the books her sister never once let her touch alone.
